{%- doc -%}
  The metal a product card should open on.

  Every product's featured image is yellow gold, so a grid of cards used to read as one
  long row of yellow rings. This spreads the opening metal across the metals a product
  actually sells: the dots are collapsed by tone the same way blocks/_card-product-metals.liquid
  collapses them, then one is chosen by a seed built from the product, so a card always
  opens on the same metal (nothing shifts between page loads, filters or pagination)
  while neighbouring cards land on different ones.

  Silver is left out of that rotation. It is the entry variant across this catalogue, so
  it sits first in nearly every option list and the card was opening a 2.3 CTW diamond
  eternity band on sterling silver. It stays a clickable swatch and it still wins when
  the shopper filters to it; it is just not a metal a card opens on by itself.

  A metal filter beats the rotation. When the shopper has narrowed the grid to one metal
  — from the menu's Shop by Metal, which links at a filtered collection
  ('menu-static-items'), or from the Metal group in the filter drawer — every card opens
  on that metal instead, so the grid shows the metal the shopper asked for rather than
  shuffling a rose gold card into a Yellow Gold result.

  Outputs "<option index>||<option value>" for the chosen metal, or nothing when the
  product has no metal option, sells only one metal, or sells nothing but silver —
  callers then keep the product's own featured image.

  @param {object} card_product - Product Liquid object

  Usage:
  {%- capture default_metal -%}{%- render 'card-default-metal', card_product: card_product -%}{%- endcapture -%}
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign option_keys = 'metal,material,color,colour,finish,gold' | split: ','

  assign metal_source = ''
  assign metal_index = -1
  for option in card_product.options_with_values
    assign option_name = option.name | downcase
    assign is_metal_option = false
    for key in option_keys
      if option_name contains key
        assign is_metal_option = true
        break
      endif
    endfor
    if is_metal_option
      assign metal_source = option.values | join: '||'
      assign metal_index = forloop.index0
      break
    endif
  endfor

  assign metal_values_raw = metal_source | split: '||'

  # The metal the grid is filtered to, if any. Liquid cannot read the query string, so
  # this comes from the filters Shopify reports as applied: a tag filter carries the
  # "metal:<colour>" tags tag-products.mjs writes, an option filter carries the option
  # value ("14K Yellow Gold"). Other groups are skipped by name, or a Rose Quartz stone
  # filter would turn the whole grid rose gold. With two metals filtered at once the
  # first of them wins for every card, which is as close as one photo can get.
  assign applied_filters = collection.filters
  if applied_filters == blank
    assign applied_filters = search.filters
  endif

  assign filtered_tone = ''
  for applied_filter in applied_filters
    assign filter_label = applied_filter.label | downcase
    assign filter_names_metal = false
    if filter_label contains 'metal' or filter_label contains 'finish' or filter_label contains 'material'
      assign filter_names_metal = true
    endif

    for applied_value in applied_filter.active_values
      assign filter_text = applied_value.value | downcase
      unless filter_names_metal or filter_text contains 'metal:'
        continue
      endunless
      if filter_text contains 'rose' or filter_text contains 'pink'
        assign filtered_tone = 'rg'
      elsif filter_text contains 'white'
        assign filtered_tone = 'wg'
      elsif filter_text contains 'yellow'
        assign filtered_tone = 'yg'
      elsif filter_text contains 'platinum'
        assign filtered_tone = 'pl'
      elsif filter_text contains 'silver'
        assign filtered_tone = 'ss'
      elsif filter_text contains 'gold'
        assign filtered_tone = 'yg'
      endif
      if filtered_tone != ''
        break
      endif
    endfor

    if filtered_tone != ''
      break
    endif
  endfor

  # Same collapsing as the metals row: every karat of white gold is one dot, and the
  # first value of each tone is kept because that is the one with a variant behind it.
  assign seen_tones = '|'
  assign metal_unique = ''
  assign metal_rotation_source = ''
  assign filtered_pick = ''
  for value in metal_values_raw
    assign dedupe_text = value | strip | downcase
    if dedupe_text contains 'rose' or dedupe_text contains 'pink'
      assign dedupe_tone = 'rg'
    elsif dedupe_text contains 'white'
      assign dedupe_tone = 'wg'
    elsif dedupe_text contains 'yellow'
      assign dedupe_tone = 'yg'
    elsif dedupe_text contains 'platinum'
      assign dedupe_tone = 'pl'
    elsif dedupe_text contains 'silver'
      assign dedupe_tone = 'ss'
    elsif dedupe_text contains 'gold'
      assign dedupe_tone = 'yg'
    else
      assign dedupe_tone = dedupe_text | handle
    endif
    assign tone_key = '|' | append: dedupe_tone | append: '|'
    unless seen_tones contains tone_key
      assign seen_tones = seen_tones | append: dedupe_tone | append: '|'
      assign metal_unique = metal_unique | append: '||' | append: value
      # Silver is the entry variant on most of this catalogue -- it is what "From £360"
      # is quoting -- so it sits first in the option list and the rotation kept opening
      # diamond pieces on it. It stays a swatch the shopper can click, but it is not a
      # metal a card opens on; the rotation runs over the golds and platinum instead.
      unless dedupe_tone == 'ss'
        assign metal_rotation_source = metal_rotation_source | append: '||' | append: value
      endunless
      # The filtered metal is read off the same pass rather than tested again below:
      # this is the first value of its tone, so it is the one with a variant behind it.
      if dedupe_tone == filtered_tone and filtered_pick == ''
        assign filtered_pick = value | strip
      endif
    endunless
  endfor

  assign metal_values = metal_unique | remove_first: '||' | split: '||'
  assign metal_rotation = metal_rotation_source | remove_first: '||' | split: '||'
-%}
{%- if metal_index >= 0 and filtered_pick != '' -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    No seed and no size test: the shopper asked for this metal, so a product that sells
    it opens on it even when it sells nothing else — the one metal it has is still worth
    pointing the photo and the link at, rather than falling back to the featured image.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {{- metal_index }}||{{ filtered_pick -}}
{%- elsif metal_index >= 0 and metal_values.size > 1 and metal_rotation.size > 0 -%}
  {%- liquid
    # The product id alone is no good as a seed: this catalogue was imported in bulk, so
    # Shopify handed out ids in a regular stride. Measured against the live catalogue the
    # gap between any two ids is a multiple of 32768 -- every id ends in the same low 15
    # bits -- so `id | modulo: 4` picked White Gold for the whole grid, and dividing by a
    # number that does not clear that stride (`divided_by: 7`) only hides it. Dividing by
    # the stride itself leaves the part of the id that actually counts products, which is
    # what the pick runs on.
    #
    # The title's length used to be added on top to separate ids sitting close together.
    # It does the opposite: a product family shares a title shape, so its lengths land on
    # the same remainder and cancel the id term for the whole family -- Trending Styles
    # came out rose, rose, rose, yellow, yellow, rose with no white gold in seven cards.
    # Checked against the live catalogue without it: every metal in the pool comes up,
    # the three tones split 10/8/7, and the carousel opens on three different metals.
    assign seed = card_product.id | divided_by: 32768
    assign pick_position = seed | modulo: metal_rotation.size
    assign picked = metal_rotation[pick_position] | strip
  -%}
  {{- metal_index }}||{{ picked -}}
{%- endif -%}
